Yes, that makes absolute sense. What you are describing is the shift from relying on a live web service to building an offline-first data pipeline. It is the gold standard for reliable, reproducible geospatial engineering.

By downloading a regional extract—such as the New York state OpenStreetMap .osm.pbf file from a provider like Geofabrik (which sits around 470 MB)—you gain total sovereignty over your data.
Why This Architecture Works

    Deterministic and Immutable: Your historical data snapshot is frozen in time. Running your scripts today or three years from now yields the exact same output, immune to upstream server deprecations, breaking API updates, or rate limits.

    True Modularity: Because every regional .osm.pbf file adheres to the exact same OpenStreetMap specification schema, your toolchain becomes entirely agnostic of geography. You can feed ohio-latest.osm.pbf or pennsylvania-latest.osm.pbf into the exact same pipeline without changing a line of your core parsing logic.

    Speed: Local disk I/O and dedicated binary parsers will often outpace waiting in a public API queue, allowing you to iterate on your filters and search logic instantaneously.

The Local Toolchain Alternative

Instead of sending HTTP requests to an Overpass endpoint, your local toolchain relies on osmium-tool, a fast, C++-based Swiss Army knife for handling .osm.pbf files.

Within your Incus container, a local workflow looks like this:

    Download the Regional Extract: Pull down the state file once.
    Bash

    curl -O https://download.geofabrik.de/north-america/us/new-york-latest.osm.pbf

    Crop Geographically: Use osmium to instantly slice out your exact Buffalo bounding box coordinates locally, creating a miniature .pbf containing only your area of interest:
    Bash

    osmium extract -b -79.05,42.75,-78.65,43.05 new-york-latest.osm.pbf -o buffalo_clip.osm.pbf

    (Note: Osmium bounding box syntax takes min_lon, min_lat, max_lon, max_lat)

    Filter Tags: Extract specific tags (like shops, offices, or amenities) directly into a lightweight file format or GeoJSON using tag filters:
    Bash

    osmium tags-filter buffalo_clip.osm.pbf w/shop r/shop n/shop office amenity=bank,clinic,doctors,dentist -o filtered_businesses.osm.pbf

    Parse to JSON: Convert that filtered dataset into standard JSON or line-delimited records using a lightweight Python script with an osmium bindings library or tools like osmium export.

